# Today's Learning Progress — Web Development

## Topics Covered

### 1. HTML Structure and Multiple Sections
- Used `<main>` and `<section>` to organize different parts of a webpage.
- Created separate full-screen sections for background videos.
- Learned how normal document flow allows sections to appear one after another.
- Understood why keeping the second section outside the first `<main>` helps with normal scrolling.

### 2. CSS Positioning
- `position: relative` keeps an element in the normal document flow and creates a positioning reference for absolute children.
- `position: absolute` positions an element relative to its nearest positioned ancestor and removes it from normal document flow.
- `position: fixed` positions an element relative to the viewport and removes it from normal document flow.
- Used relative positioning on video sections and absolute positioning for background videos.

### 3. Background Video Styling
- Used `object-fit: cover` to fill a section without stretching the video.
- Used `width: 100%` and `height: 100%` to fill the parent container.
- Used `overflow: hidden` to prevent video content from extending visibly outside its section.
- Used `muted`, `loop`, and `playsinline` attributes for background videos.

### 4. CSS Overflow and Scrollbars
Learned the difference between:

| Property | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `overflow: hidden` | Clips overflowing content |
| `overflow: auto` | Adds scrolling when needed |
| `overflow: scroll` | Provides scrolling even when not needed |
| `overflow-y: auto` | Handles vertical overflow |

Also learned how to customize browser scrollbars:

```css
::-webkit-scrollbar {
  width: 10px;
}

::-webkit-scrollbar-track {
  background: #f1f1f1;
}

::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
  background: #888;
}

::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover {
  background: #555;
}
```

### 5. Smooth Scrolling
- Learned that `scroll-behavior: smooth` makes navigation and programmatic scrolling smoother.
- Understood that it does not make ordinary mouse-wheel scrolling smooth.
- Learned about JavaScript's `scrollIntoView()` method.

Example:

```javascript
document.querySelector("#contact").scrollIntoView({
  behavior: "smooth"
});
```

### 6. JavaScript classList
Practiced dynamically changing CSS classes using JavaScript.

```javascript
element.classList.add("active");
element.classList.remove("active");
element.classList.toggle("active");
element.classList.contains("active");
```

- `add()` adds a class.
- `remove()` removes a class.
- `toggle()` adds or removes a class depending on whether it exists.
- `contains()` checks whether a class exists and returns a Boolean (`true` or `false`).

### 7. Scroll Locking During the Loader
Learned how to prevent users from scrolling while the loading screen is active.

HTML:

```html
<body class="loading">
```

CSS:

```css
body.loading {
  overflow: hidden;
}
```

JavaScript:

```javascript
document.body.classList.remove("loading");
```

Removing the class allows scrolling again after the loading process finishes.

